# DIFC third-party disclosure jurisdiction

Sources (each pinned in `difc_third_party_disclosure_source.yaml`):
  - DIFC Courts Law (Law No. 2 of 2025), Articles 15(1) and 24(D).
  - DIFC Law of Damages and Remedies (Law No. 7 of 2005), Article 36.
  - Rules of the DIFC Courts (RDC) 25.1(10), 28.51 and 28.52.
  - *Norwich Pharmacal Co v Customs and Excise Commissioners*
    [1974] AC 133 (HL).
  - *Bankers Trust Co v Shapira* [1980] 1 WLR 1274 (CA).

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md):
- DIFC Courts Law and DIFC Law of Damages and Remedies: published by
  Dubai government; reproduced for academic / research purposes.
- DIFC RDC 25.1(10), 28.51, 28.52: © DIFC Courts; reproduced under
  fair-dealing for research, with attribution, in a non-commercial
  academic working paper.
- *Norwich Pharmacal* and *Bankers Trust*: UK case law; contains
  public-sector information licensed under the Open Government
  Licence v3.0 — http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/.

## Operative provisions — verbatim text

### DIFC Courts Law (Law No. 2 of 2025), Article 15

Reproduced verbatim from Dubai Law No. 2 of 2025:

"Article (15)
The DIFC Courts have jurisdiction to hear and determine applications
for interim or precautionary measures related to the following:

1. Claims and applications that fall within the jurisdiction of the
   DIFC Courts.

2. A claim for the disclosure of the true identity of a defendant or
   a potential defendant, against whom a claim may be brought before
   the DIFC Courts.

3. A claim for the disclosure of funds or assets owned by the
   defendant or applicant in applications and claims that fall within
   the jurisdiction of the DIFC Courts.

4. Applications, claims, or current or future arbitral proceedings
   brought outside the DIFC seeking suitable precautionary measures
   within the DIFC."

Article 15(2) is the codified Norwich Pharmacal jurisdiction (identity
disclosure); Article 15(3) is the codified Bankers Trust jurisdiction
(funds/asset disclosure). The 2025 Law brings what were previously
common-law-only doctrines into express DIFC statute.

### DIFC Courts Law (Law No. 2 of 2025), Article 24(D)

Reproduced verbatim:

"D. The DIFC Court has the power to make orders as to the conduct of
any proceedings before the Court that it considers appropriate,
including but not limited to:

  1. orders prescribed by any legislation issued under the DIFC Laws;
  2. injunctions, including orders to perform an act;
  3. interim or interlocutory orders as stipulated in the DIFC Laws;
  4. orders made without notice to any other party, where required
     under the circumstances;
  5. contempt orders;
  6. orders made for the proper administration of justice; or
  7. referral of matters that constitute a crime under the
     legislation in force in the Emirate to the Attorney General of
     Dubai."

Article 24(D)(2) and 24(D)(6) supply the procedural power to make the
disclosure order itself once Article 15 jurisdiction is established.

### *Norwich Pharmacal Co v Customs and Excise Commissioners* [1974] AC 133

Lord Reid's canonical formulation:

"if through no fault of his own a person gets mixed up in the tortious
acts of others so as to facilitate their wrongdoing he may incur no
personal liability but he comes under a duty to assist the person who
has been wronged by giving him full information and disclosing the
identity of the wrongdoers."

The doctrine yields four conjunctive elements (as applied in
post-*Norwich Pharmacal* practice and codified in DIFC Article 15(2)):

  (i)   a wrong has been or arguably has been carried out;
  (ii)  the respondent (the disclosure target) has been mixed up in
        that wrong, however innocently;
  (iii) the respondent is likely to have or be able to obtain
        information necessary to identify the wrongdoer or to
        characterise the wrong; and
  (iv)  disclosure is necessary in the interests of justice.

### *Bankers Trust Co v Shapira* [1980] 1 WLR 1274 (CA)

Lord Denning MR's operative reasoning:

"In order to enable justice to be done — in order to enable these
funds to be traced — it is a very important part of the court's
armoury to be able to order discovery."

The doctrine extends *Norwich Pharmacal* to a tracing context: where
proceeds of fraud have been paid into a bank account, the court may
order the bank to disclose the state of the account and related
documents to assist the victim in tracing. As applied in DIFC and
codified in Article 15(3), the doctrine yields three conjunctive
elements:

  (i)   a tracing claim is asserted (i.e. the applicant alleges
        proprietary entitlement to identifiable proceeds);
  (ii)  the respondent (typically a financial institution) holds
        traceable proceeds or documents tending to identify them; and
  (iii) disclosure is necessary for the tracing.

### Rules of the DIFC Courts (RDC) 25.1(10), 28.51 and 28.52

Reproduced verbatim:

"25.1 The Court may grant the following kinds of interim remedies:
…
(10) an order for production of documents or inspection of property
against a non-party"

"28.51 An application for production of documents by a person who is
not a party to the proceedings must under these Rules be supported by
evidence."

"28.52 The Court may make an order under this rule only where:
  (1) the documents of which production is sought are likely to
      support the case of the applicant or adversely affect the case
      of one of the other parties to the proceedings; and
  (2) production is necessary in order to dispose fairly of the claim
      or to save costs."

### DIFC Law of Damages and Remedies (Law No. 7 of 2005), Article 36

Article 36 confers the substantive damages-and-tracing remedies that
the disclosure order assists. It is the underlying right to which
*Bankers Trust* relief is ancillary; without a tracing claim grounded
in Article 36 (or an analogous statutory or common-law claim), the
*Bankers Trust* gate is unavailable.

## Structural map

The DIFC Courts have three independent jurisdictional gates by which
they can order disclosure against a non-party. ALL three must be made
out for the Court to reach over to the non-party. Each gate is itself
conjunctive over its own elements.

*Gate 1 — Norwich Pharmacal* (codified in DIFC Courts Law Art 15(2);
underlying doctrine [1974] AC 133). Four conjunctive elements:
  NPE_WrongEstablished
  NPE_RespondentMixedUp
  NPE_PossessesInformation
  NPE_DisclosureNecessaryInInterestsOfJustice

*Gate 2 — Bankers Trust* (codified in DIFC Courts Law Art 15(3);
applied via DIFC Law of Damages and Remedies Art 36 for the tracing
claim itself; underlying doctrine [1980] 1 WLR 1274). Three
conjunctive elements:
  BTE_TracingClaimAsserted
  BTE_HoldsTraceableProceeds
  BTE_DisclosureNecessaryForTracing

*Gate 3 — RDC 28.52* (procedural conditions). Two conjunctive
conditions:
  RDC_LikelyToSupportOrAffect
  RDC_NecessaryToDisposeOrSaveCosts

## Encoding

```catala
declaration enumeration NorwichPharmacalElement:
  -- NPE_WrongEstablished
  -- NPE_RespondentMixedUp
  -- NPE_PossessesInformation
  -- NPE_DisclosureNecessaryInInterestsOfJustice

declaration enumeration BankersTrustElement:
  -- BTE_TracingClaimAsserted
  -- BTE_HoldsTraceableProceeds
  -- BTE_DisclosureNecessaryForTracing

declaration enumeration RDC2852Condition:
  -- RDC_LikelyToSupportOrAffect
  -- RDC_NecessaryToDisposeOrSaveCosts

declaration structure NPFinding:
  data element content NorwichPharmacalElement
  data made_out content boolean

declaration structure BTFinding:
  data element content BankersTrustElement
  data made_out content boolean

declaration structure RDCFinding:
  data rdc_condition content RDC2852Condition
  data made_out content boolean

declaration structure DisclosureGates:
  data nph_made_out content boolean
  data bankers_trust_made_out content boolean
  data rdc_2852_made_out content boolean
  data all_gates_satisfied content boolean
  data order_grantable content boolean

declaration scope ThirdPartyDisclosureGates:
  input nph_findings content list of NPFinding
  input bt_findings content list of BTFinding
  input rdc_findings content list of RDCFinding
  output gates content DisclosureGates

scope ThirdPartyDisclosureGates:
  definition gates equals
    ## Each gate requires every named enum value to appear with
    ## made_out=true. This formulation:
    ##   - rejects empty input (vacuous-satisfaction bug);
    ##   - tolerates duplicates (the same element claimed twice
    ##     made_out=true counts once);
    ##   - tolerates over-pleading (extra entries do not break the
    ##     gate);
    ##   - is resilient to enum evolution (adding a 5th NPh element
    ##     merely adds another conjunct here, with the matching
    ##     source-yaml drift check flagging the change).
    let nph equals
      (exists f among nph_findings such that
        f.element = NPE_WrongEstablished and f.made_out)
      and (exists f among nph_findings such that
        f.element = NPE_RespondentMixedUp and f.made_out)
      and (exists f among nph_findings such that
        f.element = NPE_PossessesInformation and f.made_out)
      and (exists f among nph_findings such that
        f.element = NPE_DisclosureNecessaryInInterestsOfJustice
        and f.made_out)
    in
    let bt equals
      (exists f among bt_findings such that
        f.element = BTE_TracingClaimAsserted and f.made_out)
      and (exists f among bt_findings such that
        f.element = BTE_HoldsTraceableProceeds and f.made_out)
      and (exists f among bt_findings such that
        f.element = BTE_DisclosureNecessaryForTracing and f.made_out)
    in
    let rdc equals
      (exists f among rdc_findings such that
        f.rdc_condition = RDC_LikelyToSupportOrAffect and f.made_out)
      and (exists f among rdc_findings such that
        f.rdc_condition = RDC_NecessaryToDisposeOrSaveCosts
        and f.made_out)
    in
    let all_gates equals nph and bt and rdc in
    DisclosureGates {
      -- nph_made_out: nph
      -- bankers_trust_made_out: bt
      -- rdc_2852_made_out: rdc
      -- all_gates_satisfied: all_gates
      -- order_grantable: all_gates
    }
```

## Used in

trace-07 — DEC 001/2025 *Techteryx Ltd v IG Limited* (DIFC Digital
Economy Court, Black KC, 3 April 2026).

The trace-07 rule file embeds the same core inline alongside the
case-specific test scope; this rule module is what the Stage-2
simulator consumes.

## Tests

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestThirdPartyDisclosureGranted:
  composite scope ThirdPartyDisclosureGates
  output gates content DisclosureGates

scope TestThirdPartyDisclosureGranted:
  definition gates equals composite.gates
  ## Trace-07 facts: every element of every gate made out.
  definition composite.nph_findings equals [
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_WrongEstablished                       -- made_out: true };
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_RespondentMixedUp                      -- made_out: true };
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_PossessesInformation                   -- made_out: true };
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_DisclosureNecessaryInInterestsOfJustice -- made_out: true }
  ]
  definition composite.bt_findings equals [
    BTFinding { -- element: BTE_TracingClaimAsserted        -- made_out: true };
    BTFinding { -- element: BTE_HoldsTraceableProceeds      -- made_out: true };
    BTFinding { -- element: BTE_DisclosureNecessaryForTracing -- made_out: true }
  ]
  definition composite.rdc_findings equals [
    RDCFinding { -- rdc_condition: RDC_LikelyToSupportOrAffect       -- made_out: true };
    RDCFinding { -- rdc_condition: RDC_NecessaryToDisposeOrSaveCosts -- made_out: true }
  ]
  assertion gates.order_grantable = true

#[test] declaration scope TestThirdPartyDisclosureRefused:
  composite scope ThirdPartyDisclosureGates
  output gates content DisclosureGates

scope TestThirdPartyDisclosureRefused:
  definition gates equals composite.gates
  ## A single missing Norwich Pharmacal element is dispositive.
  definition composite.nph_findings equals [
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_WrongEstablished                       -- made_out: true };
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_RespondentMixedUp                      -- made_out: false };
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_PossessesInformation                   -- made_out: true };
    NPFinding { -- element: NPE_DisclosureNecessaryInInterestsOfJustice -- made_out: true }
  ]
  definition composite.bt_findings equals [
    BTFinding { -- element: BTE_TracingClaimAsserted        -- made_out: true };
    BTFinding { -- element: BTE_HoldsTraceableProceeds      -- made_out: true };
    BTFinding { -- element: BTE_DisclosureNecessaryForTracing -- made_out: true }
  ]
  definition composite.rdc_findings equals [
    RDCFinding { -- rdc_condition: RDC_LikelyToSupportOrAffect       -- made_out: true };
    RDCFinding { -- rdc_condition: RDC_NecessaryToDisposeOrSaveCosts -- made_out: true }
  ]
  assertion gates.order_grantable = false
  assertion gates.nph_made_out = false
```
